WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.560 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A delivery bicycle must travel from the library to a
community garden.

00:00:04.560 --> 00:00:04.880 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
One rider turns early and another turns later, but both
insist they used the shortest grid route.

00:00:04.880 --> 00:00:13.240 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Can different-looking journeys have the same length?

00:00:13.240 --> 00:00:17.038 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Let us become route planners and check their claims with a
map.

00:00:17.038 --> 00:00:17.518 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Today we are learning to describe and compare routes on a
coordinate grid.

00:00:17.518 --> 00:00:27.539 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
We will build an explanation using models, rather than
memorising a rule without understanding it.

00:00:27.539 --> 00:00:31.779 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Our learning goal is to describe and compare routes on a
coordinate grid.

00:00:31.779 --> 00:00:37.442 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
As you investigate, connect what you can see, what you can
make, and the mathematical language you use.

00:00:37.442 --> 00:00:41.122 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
First, I can follow horizontal and vertical movements.

00:00:41.122 --> 00:00:44.162 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Second, I can connect movements to coordinates.

00:00:44.162 --> 00:00:47.522 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Third, I can compare routes and explain my reasoning.

00:00:47.522 --> 00:00:50.978 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Keep these goals in mind as you explain each discovery.

00:00:50.978 --> 00:00:52.658 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A coordinate pair names a position.

00:00:52.658 --> 00:00:56.898 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A route describes how to travel between positions.

00:00:56.898 --> 00:01:00.578 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
These ideas are connected, but they answer different
questions.

00:01:00.578 --> 00:01:09.489 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
First identify the starting point and destination, then
describe each movement clearly enough for another person to
follow without watching your finger.

00:01:09.489 --> 00:01:12.969 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
On this map, right means east and up means north.

00:01:12.969 --> 00:01:15.609 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Left means west and down means south.

00:01:15.609 --> 00:01:19.929 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The grid axes still describe horizontal and vertical
position.

00:01:19.929 --> 00:01:27.964 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Before using these directions on a real map, check its
orientation rather than assuming the top of every page must
represent north.

00:01:27.964 --> 00:01:31.564 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The library is at one, one and the garden is at five, four.

00:01:31.564 --> 00:01:32.204 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
To compare the positions, look at each coordinate
separately.

00:01:32.204 --> 00:01:35.124 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The horizontal value must increase from one to five.

00:01:35.124 --> 00:01:41.804 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The vertical value must increase from one to four.

00:01:41.804 --> 00:01:46.101 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
We can describe these two required changes before choosing
turns.

00:01:46.101 --> 00:01:48.781 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
From one, one, move four spaces right.

00:01:48.781 --> 00:01:50.981 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The new position is five, one.

00:01:50.981 --> 00:01:55.301 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The vertical coordinate stays one because we have not moved
up or down.

00:01:55.301 --> 00:01:58.421 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Then move three spaces up to reach five, four.

00:01:58.421 --> 00:02:02.931 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Each intermediate position gives us a useful check on the
directions.

00:02:02.931 --> 00:02:09.491 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
This journey uses four horizontal spaces and three vertical
spaces, giving seven grid units altogether.

00:02:09.491 --> 00:02:11.691 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
We are counting travelled spaces, not the labels at their
ends.

00:02:11.691 --> 00:02:18.993 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The turn does not add an extra unit: it changes the
direction of the next part of the journey.

00:02:18.993 --> 00:02:24.353 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
What if the second rider goes three spaces north before
moving four spaces east?

00:02:24.353 --> 00:02:27.833 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Predict the final position and total grid distance.

00:02:27.833 --> 00:02:28.153 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Think about which coordinate changes during each part.

00:02:28.153 --> 00:02:37.693 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Does changing the order of these two movements change the
destination in this particular situation?

00:02:42.693 --> 00:02:48.133 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The second rider reaches one, four after moving north, then
five, four after moving east.

00:02:48.133 --> 00:02:51.733 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The distance is three plus four, also seven grid units.

00:02:51.733 --> 00:02:57.013 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The routes use different intermediate positions but have the
same destination and distance.

00:02:57.013 --> 00:03:01.432 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
This conclusion follows from the particular movements, not
from their appearance.

00:03:01.432 --> 00:03:06.632 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Now the bicycle must continue from the garden at five, four
to a gate at seven, four.

00:03:06.632 --> 00:03:10.152 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
That adds two spaces east with no vertical change.

00:03:10.152 --> 00:03:13.592 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The complete route uses four right, three up and two right.

00:03:13.592 --> 00:03:17.891 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Keeping a running record helps us avoid forgetting an
earlier section.

00:03:17.891 --> 00:03:21.331 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Record the start, each instruction and the new position.

00:03:21.331 --> 00:03:23.731 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
After four right, write five, one.

00:03:23.731 --> 00:03:26.051 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
After three up, write five, four.

00:03:26.051 --> 00:03:28.291 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
After two right, write seven, four.

00:03:28.291 --> 00:03:34.333 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The travelled distance is four plus three plus two, which
equals nine grid units for this complete journey.

00:03:34.333 --> 00:03:38.493 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A learner adds seven and four and says the journey was
eleven units.

00:03:38.493 --> 00:03:41.693 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Those values describe the final position relative to the
origin, not the distance travelled from our library.

00:03:41.693 --> 00:03:51.578 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
To measure the journey, use the movements along the actual
route, including any sections that retrace earlier steps.

00:03:51.578 --> 00:03:53.338 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Suppose a closed path makes the rider travel one extra space
east and then one space back west before continuing.

00:03:53.338 --> 00:04:04.778 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The final destination can remain unchanged, but the rider
has travelled two additional grid units.

00:04:04.778 --> 00:04:10.138 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Returning to the same position does not erase the distance
already travelled along a detour.

00:04:10.138 --> 00:04:17.858 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Among routes restricted to horizontal and vertical grid
lines, seven units is a shortest distance from one, one to
five, four.

00:04:17.858 --> 00:04:22.938 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Every successful route must cover four units of eastward
change and three northward.

00:04:22.938 --> 00:04:29.420 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Different orders can achieve this without backtracking, so
shortest does not mean there is only one possible route.

00:04:29.420 --> 00:04:32.940 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A walker starts at two, two and ends at six, five.

00:04:32.940 --> 00:04:36.140 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The first instruction is four spaces right.

00:04:36.140 --> 00:04:38.820 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
What vertical movement completes the trip?

00:04:38.820 --> 00:04:43.540 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Predict the instruction and explain why it changes only one
coordinate.

00:04:43.540 --> 00:04:48.389 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Check the starting height instead of reading the
destination’s height as the distance.

00:04:53.389 --> 00:04:56.269 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
After moving right, the walker is at six, two.

00:04:56.269 --> 00:04:57.149 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
To reach six, five, move three spaces up.

00:04:57.149 --> 00:05:02.549 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Five minus two gives the required vertical change.

00:05:02.549 --> 00:05:09.729 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Moving five spaces up would reach height seven, because the
starting point was already two units above the horizontal
axis.

00:05:09.729 --> 00:05:13.369 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Imagine that each grid unit represents ten metres.

00:05:13.369 --> 00:05:19.089 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Our seven-unit library-to-garden route then represents
seventy metres along the grid paths.

00:05:19.089 --> 00:05:21.729 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
State the scale and units in your answer.

00:05:21.729 --> 00:05:28.718 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A distance of seven grid units is not automatically seven
metres unless the map explicitly uses that scale.

00:05:28.718 --> 00:05:33.558 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Write a new route using at least three instructions and
include its start position.

00:05:33.558 --> 00:05:37.718 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Ask a partner to follow it, recording each intermediate
coordinate.

00:05:37.718 --> 00:05:42.558 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
If the final location is unexpected, compare one instruction
at a time.

00:05:42.558 --> 00:05:47.564 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
This pinpoints the first disagreement instead of rewriting
the whole route by guesswork.

00:05:47.564 --> 00:05:51.724 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Plan two different shortest grid routes from two, two to
six, five.

00:05:51.724 --> 00:05:58.124 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Both need four units of eastward change and three northward,
so each can use seven units without backtracking.

00:05:58.124 --> 00:06:02.445 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Explain how their turns differ while the total changes
remain the same.

00:06:02.445 --> 00:06:05.061 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Include a labelled sketch as evidence.

00:06:05.061 --> 00:06:08.741 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
You can now connect positions, movements and travelled
distance.

00:06:08.741 --> 00:06:16.741 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Two riders can take different turns yet share a shortest
grid distance, while a detour can change distance without
changing the destination.

00:06:16.741 --> 00:06:22.779 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Make one final route clue and explain how a coordinate
record would help someone check every stage.
